The program is written in C language of C11 standard using gcc compiler.
The program consists of two parts: a library implementing the logic of the tetris game, and a terminal interface using the ncurses library.
A finite-state machine is used to formalize the logic of the game.
The library has a function that accepts user input and a function that outputs a matrix that describes the current state of the playing field whenever it is changed.
The program library code is located in the src/brick_game/tetris folder.
The program interface code is located in the src/gui/cli folder
The program is built using a Makefile with the standard set of targets for GNU-programs: all, install, uninstall, clean, dvi, dist, test, gcov_report. Installation directory can be arbitrary
The program is developed in accordance with the principles of structured programming.
Prepared full coverage of the library with unit tests, using the check library. The coverage of the library with game logic with tests is more than 80 percent.
The following mechanics is in the game:

Rotation of pieces;
Moving pieces horizontally;
Acceleration of the piece's fall (when the button is pressed, the figure moves all the way down);
Display of the next piece;
Destruction of filled raws;
End of the game when the top border of the playing field is reached;
All sorts of pieces shown in the picture below must be included in the game.

There is the support for all the buttons provided on the physical console for control:

Start game - S botton
Pause - Space
End game - Esc
Left arrow
Right arrow
Down arrow - piece falls
Up arrow is not used in this game
Action (piece rotation) - Enter.


The playing field matches the dimensions of the console's playing field - ten "pixels" wide and twenty "pixels" high.
After reaching the lower boundary of the field or contacting another figure, the figure stops. After that, the next piece, shown in the preview, is generated.
The library interface corresponds to the description found in materials/library-specification.md.
The UI supports rendering of the playing field and additional information.


Bonus. Scoring and game record
The game has next mechanics scoring, storing maximum points.

This information is displayed by the user interface in the sidebar. The maximum number of points is stored in a file and saved between program runs.
The maximum number of points ischanged during the game if the user exceeds the current maximum score.
Points is accrued as follows:

1 row is 100 points;
2 rows is 300 points;
3 rows is 700 points;
4 rows is 1500 points;


Bonus. Level mechanics
The game has level mechanics. Each time a player gains 600 points, the level increases by 1. Increasing the level boosts the speed at which the pieces move. The maximum number of levels is 10.


